1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
Our world is falling apart.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:07,040
Bridges collapse.

3
00:00:07,041 --> 00:00:08,760
Pipelines explode.

4
00:00:08,761 --> 00:00:10,400
Buildings decay.

5
00:00:10,401 --> 00:00:12,840
It's an all-out war on rust and rot.

6
00:00:12,841 --> 00:00:15,400
This is a massive generational undertaking

7
00:00:15,401 --> 00:00:19,240
of what caused billions of dollars over several decades.

8
00:00:19,241 --> 00:00:23,280
The Navy has taken the battle indoors,

9
00:00:23,281 --> 00:00:27,480
while robots take on pipeline intruders.

10
00:00:27,481 --> 00:00:30,240
And automakers dream up new torture tests for vehicles.

11
00:00:30,540 --> 00:00:33,480
It takes roughly 20 weeks to simulate 10 years of corrosion.

12
00:00:34,220 --> 00:00:37,280
But mountains of decaying garbage produce energy.

13
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:41,360
This is like the epicenter of world bioreactor research.

14
00:00:41,820 --> 00:00:45,360
And the termite that attacks 600,000 homes each year

15
00:00:45,361 --> 00:00:47,960
may lead to an energy promised land.

16
00:00:48,760 --> 00:00:52,340
Now, corrosion and decomposition on Modern Marbles.

17
00:00:57,480 --> 00:01:11,480
To chronicle the failing infrastructure in the United States,

18
00:01:17,860 --> 00:01:20,780
every four years, the American Society of Civil Engineers

19
00:01:20,781 --> 00:01:22,360
issues a report card.

20
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:25,080
The report card on America's infrastructure

21
00:01:25,081 --> 00:01:28,880
breaks the country's infrastructure into 15 categories,

22
00:01:29,060 --> 00:01:33,600
ranging from bridges, airports, water, wastewater, sewers,

23
00:01:34,080 --> 00:01:38,300
and gives each of them a grade similar to what someone would get

24
00:01:38,301 --> 00:01:40,280
in a report card in school from A to F.

25
00:01:40,800 --> 00:01:44,980
ASE has given the nation's infrastructure an overall grade of D.

26
00:01:46,500 --> 00:01:50,580
The primary cause for our failing infrastructure, corrosion.

27
00:01:51,460 --> 00:01:57,140
All of the categories that were rated by ASC for the infrastructure are affected by corrosion.

28
00:01:57,480 --> 00:02:00,420
It's lack of maintenance that shortens the life of infrastructure.

29
00:02:02,600 --> 00:02:06,600
Corrosion costs the U.S. economy $300 billion each year.

30
00:02:08,380 --> 00:02:13,560
That's 4% of our gross domestic product, nearly half of what we spend on foreign oil,

31
00:02:13,561 --> 00:02:17,280
or roughly $1,000 per American citizen annually.

32
00:02:17,281 --> 00:02:22,280
Corrosion's destructive power can be found everywhere,

33
00:02:22,281 --> 00:02:29,280
from rusting railroad trestles to deteriorating oil pipelines.

34
00:02:29,281 --> 00:02:34,280
But what exactly causes this scourge that eats away at our society?

35
00:02:34,281 --> 00:02:37,280
Many different environments can cause corrosion.

36
00:02:37,281 --> 00:02:41,280
A corrosive environment can be seawater, can be the atmosphere,

37
00:02:41,281 --> 00:02:44,280
can be a chemical solution, an acid.

38
00:02:44,281 --> 00:02:51,280
It's a natural electrochemical reaction occurring on an atomic level in most metals.

39
00:02:51,281 --> 00:02:58,280
Water conducts electricity that allows the exchange of electrons on the surface of the metal.

40
00:02:58,281 --> 00:03:06,280
Positively charged atomic particles called ions flow from the metal and combine with the oxygen in the water to form an oxide.

41
00:03:06,281 --> 00:03:13,280
In the case of iron, that oxide is rust. Iron is found as an ore, iron oxide.

42
00:03:13,281 --> 00:03:18,280
We put energy into the ore to produce pure iron.

43
00:03:18,380 --> 00:03:21,180
When this iron is exposed to a corrosive environment,

44
00:03:21,280 --> 00:03:24,420
it rusts, releasing that stored energy

45
00:03:24,520 --> 00:03:27,920
and reverting to an oxide state.

46
00:03:28,020 --> 00:03:30,440
Different metals corrode at different rates.

47
00:03:30,540 --> 00:03:34,440
Reactive metals like iron oxidize very easily,

48
00:03:34,540 --> 00:03:37,740
while more passive metals like gold resist corrosion,

49
00:03:37,840 --> 00:03:41,680
earning them the title noble metals.

50
00:03:41,681 --> 00:03:46,160
That's why you'll find non-oxidizing metals such as platinum used in medical implants.

51
00:03:47,520 --> 00:03:51,620
But in a world with an infrastructure built primarily of iron and steel,

52
00:03:52,420 --> 00:03:55,740
corrosion is rampant, and its impact can be catastrophic.

53
00:04:02,180 --> 00:04:08,880
The I-35W collapse was a wake-up call for a lot of the nation in terms of infrastructure.

54
00:04:09,520 --> 00:04:11,560
Infrastructure has a definite lifespan.

55
00:04:12,180 --> 00:04:14,100
Corrosion can shorten the life of a bridge,

56
00:04:14,520 --> 00:04:16,740
can shorten the life of a concrete deck.

57
00:04:17,080 --> 00:04:18,520
Right now, if you look at the country,

58
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:22,360
we have roughly 12% of the bridge is structurally deficient

59
00:04:22,361 --> 00:04:25,680
and 12% of the bridge is functionally obsolete.

60
00:04:27,020 --> 00:04:28,960
The Bourne Bridge in Massachusetts

61
00:04:28,961 --> 00:04:31,300
is listed by the Federal Highway Administration

62
00:04:31,301 --> 00:04:34,500
as one of the 20 most structurally deficient in the nation.

63
00:04:34,501 --> 00:04:39,060
It's joined by the San Francisco-Oakland Bay Bridge,

64
00:04:39,380 --> 00:04:41,880
which serves a quarter of a million vehicles each day.

65
00:04:44,500 --> 00:04:47,580
The Department of Transportation also gives a poor rating

66
00:04:47,581 --> 00:04:49,260
to this aging American landmark.

67
00:04:50,120 --> 00:04:53,120
Just repairing the corroding approach ramps on the Brooklyn Bridge

68
00:04:53,121 --> 00:04:55,540
will cost $275 million.

69
00:04:56,640 --> 00:05:02,200
It was estimated in 2005 that it would cost $9.4 billion a year

70
00:05:02,201 --> 00:05:05,500
for the next 20 years to remove all of the deficiencies of our bridges.

71
00:05:06,480 --> 00:05:10,740
America's bridges may be bad, but our transit system is worse.

72
00:05:13,640 --> 00:05:19,480
It's estimated that our corroding urban rail system requires $14.8 billion annually

73
00:05:19,481 --> 00:05:22,160
just to maintain existing poor conditions.

74
00:05:23,380 --> 00:05:27,900
Right now, a lot of the departments of transportation are having to make a choice

75
00:05:27,901 --> 00:05:31,960
when it comes to either building new structures or maintaining the existing structures.

76
00:05:32,200 --> 00:05:39,200
Maintaining mass transit's corrosion-prone steel support structures requires special protective coatings.

77
00:05:39,201 --> 00:05:46,200
Zinc primers and urethane top coatings combine to preserve the steel for extended periods.

78
00:05:48,200 --> 00:05:55,200
But even coatings can't stop corrosion caused by what many transit authorities consider their worst enemy, stray current.

79
00:05:55,201 --> 00:06:01,360
It's a phenomenon commonly found in modern subway systems powered by direct current.

80
00:06:02,360 --> 00:06:06,320
In New York subway, the direct current is delivered via the third rail.

81
00:06:07,540 --> 00:06:13,080
There are 600 volts of direct current at almost unlimited amperage going through the third rail.

82
00:06:13,600 --> 00:06:15,880
If you touch it, you'll instantly be killed.

83
00:06:16,680 --> 00:06:20,140
The third rail provides direct current power to the subway train,

84
00:06:20,780 --> 00:06:25,080
then loops back through the system via the running rails, returning to the substation.

85
00:06:25,200 --> 00:06:29,800
completing a cycle. Current from the rails tends to stray into the soil,

86
00:06:30,680 --> 00:06:33,220
attracted to any metal object that might be buried nearby.

87
00:06:34,880 --> 00:06:38,800
Corrosion dramatically accelerates at the point where current leaves the buried metal object.

88
00:06:42,060 --> 00:06:45,580
Older transit systems are most susceptible to stray current corrosion

89
00:06:45,581 --> 00:06:48,400
due to general deterioration and poor insulation.

90
00:06:48,401 --> 00:06:57,040
New York City's 100-year-old Mass Transit Authority ranks corrosion as its number one maintenance issue.

91
00:06:58,440 --> 00:07:04,500
But according to the American Society of Civil Engineers, we have far bigger corrosion problems to worry about.

92
00:07:04,960 --> 00:07:10,140
The worst areas with a grade of D-minus are drinking water, wastewater.

93
00:07:10,141 --> 00:07:18,380
The Environmental Protection Agency estimates that nearly $1 trillion is needed in critical drinking water and wastewater investment

94
00:07:18,381 --> 00:07:20,140
over the next 20 years.

95
00:07:20,960 --> 00:07:23,660
The very nature of wastewater is corrosive.

96
00:07:24,040 --> 00:07:27,800
It breaks down, and that increases the acid content in wastewater.

97
00:07:28,300 --> 00:07:31,620
What that means is it essentially eats away at the pipe, weakening it.

98
00:07:32,040 --> 00:07:36,420
It becomes much more subject to leaks and structural breakdown,

99
00:07:36,700 --> 00:07:38,420
which is sewer pipe collapsing.

100
00:07:39,180 --> 00:07:41,000
Collapsed sewer pipes mean spills,

101
00:07:41,800 --> 00:07:44,640
and spills mean fines from the Environmental Protection Agency.

102
00:07:44,641 --> 00:07:51,640
Detroit, Atlanta, and Washington, D.C. have seen their fair share.

103
00:07:51,641 --> 00:07:59,640
But the worst offender is San Diego, with $6.2 million in EPA penalties between 2005 and 2008.

104
00:07:59,641 --> 00:08:06,640
Some of these structures are from the early part of the last century, and they're wearing out.

105
00:08:06,641 --> 00:08:07,651
Here in the city of St.

106
00:08:07,652 --> 00:08:10,640
Louis, we have the fourth largest sewer system in the United States,

107
00:08:10,641 --> 00:08:12,640
parts of which date back to the 1850s,

108
00:08:12,641 --> 00:08:15,640
before even when Abraham Lincoln was president.

109
00:08:15,641 --> 00:08:17,640
We have wooden sewers, actually, downtown,

110
00:08:17,641 --> 00:08:19,640
and those wooden sewers are amongst the first sewers

111
00:08:19,641 --> 00:08:21,640
that were built in the 1850s.

112
00:08:21,641 --> 00:08:24,640
But it's the infrastructure that we have today.

113
00:08:24,641 --> 00:08:26,640
The trillion-dollar price tag on our aging water

114
00:08:26,641 --> 00:08:31,640
and sewer systems may be reduced by invented new technologies,

115
00:08:31,641 --> 00:08:34,640
like those used to overhaul St. Louis' 10,000 miles

116
00:08:34,641 --> 00:08:36,640
of underground pipe.

117
00:08:36,641 --> 00:08:39,640
To save time and money, the city is rebuilding its sewers,

118
00:08:39,641 --> 00:08:41,720
building its sewers from the inside out.

119
00:08:42,180 --> 00:08:46,460
The conventional way would be to come out here with a backhoe and dig up the street and

120
00:08:46,461 --> 00:08:50,360
put in a new pipe, but that's very expensive, very time-consuming.

121
00:08:50,920 --> 00:08:53,120
In lieu of that, we have cured-in-place pipe.

122
00:08:53,720 --> 00:08:56,960
What that is, is we're working with polyester resin.

123
00:08:57,100 --> 00:08:59,180
That's the same thing that boats are made out of.

124
00:08:59,420 --> 00:09:03,620
What we're essentially doing is we're using the old pipe as a form, and we're building

125
00:09:03,621 --> 00:09:06,440
a new pipe inside that without digging up the street.

126
00:09:06,880 --> 00:09:08,680
We're going to have this done in one day.

127
00:09:09,640 --> 00:09:15,040
Trenchless repair systems developed by in situ form technologies are rapidly

128
00:09:15,041 --> 00:09:21,220
overtaking traditional dig and replace methods of pipe restoration. It starts

129
00:09:21,221 --> 00:09:25,460
with an inspection by an iBot that doesn't mind crawling through sewers.

130
00:09:25,461 --> 00:09:30,220
It allows us to get a video of the pipeline before and after we install a

131
00:09:30,221 --> 00:09:34,600
liner. It works basically on its own power. You run this through the line

132
00:09:34,601 --> 00:09:38,500
initially to make sure it's clean so when we drag our liner through there's

133
00:09:38,501 --> 00:09:40,940
There's not going to be any bumps or anything that's real ugly in there.

134
00:09:41,600 --> 00:09:47,940
It feeds basically into our TV truck, and I can see everything on a television monitor without actually having to go into the line.

135
00:09:50,080 --> 00:09:54,780
The robots aren't afraid of tight places or unexpected visitors.

136
00:09:56,300 --> 00:09:58,560
Every now and again you get to see some crazy stuff.

137
00:09:58,561 --> 00:10:07,560
In trenchless technology, instead of unearthing the corroded pipe, a resin-saturated coated

138
00:10:07,561 --> 00:10:11,560
felt tubing is inserted into it.

139
00:10:11,561 --> 00:10:17,560
Compressed air inflates the tube, extending it the length of the pipe.

140
00:10:17,561 --> 00:10:22,560
Installers then circulate steam through the tubing, curing the resin and forming a tight-fitting

141
00:10:22,561 --> 00:10:24,560
pipe within the pipe.

142
00:10:24,561 --> 00:10:26,920
What this is, it's thermal set, heat set,

143
00:10:27,020 --> 00:10:29,600
so when we raise the temperature,

144
00:10:29,740 --> 00:10:31,700
in this case to 130 degrees Fahrenheit,

145
00:10:32,360 --> 00:10:33,680
it's going to extra-therin.

146
00:10:33,740 --> 00:10:35,620
In other words, it's going to go from liquid to solid.

147
00:10:35,720 --> 00:10:36,720
It's a chemical reaction.

148
00:10:37,120 --> 00:10:39,780
It's kind of like when people buy an epoxy at the store

149
00:10:39,781 --> 00:10:41,800
and you've got two parts and you mix them together.

150
00:10:42,220 --> 00:10:43,640
There's heat generated there,

151
00:10:43,680 --> 00:10:45,020
and that goes from liquid to solid.

152
00:10:45,540 --> 00:10:48,240
The pipe's going to be solid, just like this pipe in my hand.

153
00:10:48,300 --> 00:10:49,200
This is an 8-inch.

154
00:10:49,920 --> 00:10:50,800
Here's the felt.

155
00:10:51,360 --> 00:10:54,140
This has a resin in it installed in the pipe.

156
00:10:54,560 --> 00:10:57,560
And then when we finish, it'll be a structural pipe.

157
00:10:58,940 --> 00:11:01,980
The crew relies on the iBot to finish the job.

158
00:11:02,800 --> 00:11:05,440
By attaching a movable drill to the front of the iBot,

159
00:11:06,460 --> 00:11:09,540
a technician can reopen access to individual homes,

160
00:11:09,960 --> 00:11:11,120
called service laterals,

161
00:11:11,640 --> 00:11:13,840
that have been sealed by the liner within the pipe.

162
00:11:15,600 --> 00:11:17,720
Basically what I do is when I approach a service,

163
00:11:17,880 --> 00:11:19,840
I find the dimple where the service is at.

164
00:11:20,880 --> 00:11:22,520
So this is what we're looking for.

165
00:11:22,521 --> 00:11:25,860
That's how I know what I got to cut out.

166
00:11:25,861 --> 00:11:28,520
I get my bit up to the service,

167
00:11:28,521 --> 00:11:31,820
and I will cut a hole in the middle of it.

168
00:11:31,821 --> 00:11:35,900
Generally, it takes about five minutes to cut a service.

169
00:11:35,901 --> 00:11:39,100
We can line an entire street and reinstate everybody's

170
00:11:39,101 --> 00:11:43,400
laterals to their houses and have their water flowing in one day.

171
00:11:43,401 --> 00:11:45,720
What this will allow us to do is restore this line,

172
00:11:45,721 --> 00:11:48,040
maintain service to these customers

173
00:11:48,041 --> 00:11:50,220
without tearing up several blocks of street

174
00:11:50,221 --> 00:11:51,680
and causing a major inconvenience

175
00:11:51,681 --> 00:11:54,020
for everybody who lives in this neighborhood.

176
00:11:54,021 --> 00:11:57,020
Trenchless sewer replacement costs up to 50% less

177
00:11:57,021 --> 00:11:58,920
than traditional methods.

178
00:11:58,921 --> 00:12:02,500
It works equally well with drinking water pipes.

179
00:12:02,501 --> 00:12:05,200
But not every city is as proactive as St. Louis

180
00:12:05,201 --> 00:12:07,560
in restoring their corroding sewer and water delivery

181
00:12:07,561 --> 00:12:08,860
systems.

182
00:12:08,861 --> 00:12:11,460
Many communities are faced with a countdown of sorts

183
00:12:11,461 --> 00:12:14,300
such as to which of their infrastructure

184
00:12:14,301 --> 00:12:18,480
is going to fail most rapidly and maybe even most dramatically.

185
00:12:18,481 --> 00:12:28,820
And while this underground assets tend to not be as spectacular as a bridge failing or a road failing or even a large sinkhole,

186
00:12:28,821 --> 00:12:35,860
but they can contribute mightily to environmental degradation with sewer leaks and even quality of living issues.

187
00:12:36,520 --> 00:12:41,020
If the mix of metal and water in America's infrastructure is a recipe for corrosion,

188
00:12:41,880 --> 00:12:45,700
what happens when you add salt to the equation?

189
00:12:45,701 --> 00:12:49,000
For the U.S. Navy, it's a recipe for disaster,

190
00:12:49,001 --> 00:12:53,340
unless you've got the most advanced research in the world on your side.

191
00:12:55,340 --> 00:12:59,260
According to 2007 U.S. Bureau of Transportation Statistics,

192
00:12:59,580 --> 00:13:04,080
53% of Rhode Island's bridges are structurally deficient or functionally obsolete,

193
00:13:04,400 --> 00:13:05,720
the highest rate in the nation.

194
00:13:06,180 --> 00:13:09,280
Corrosion and decomposition will return on Modern Marvels.

195
00:13:11,360 --> 00:13:14,560
The United States commands the largest Navy in the world.

196
00:13:15,700 --> 00:13:22,700
More than 280 ships and 3,700 aircraft currently patrol seas from the Far East to the Mediterranean.

197
00:13:25,400 --> 00:13:28,320
But if you're looking for the largest fleet in the Pacific Ocean,

198
00:13:28,960 --> 00:13:34,520
you'll need to go 30 miles northeast of San Francisco to the backwater port of Susun Bay.

199
00:13:35,300 --> 00:13:37,460
Most of these vessels are past their prime.

200
00:13:38,200 --> 00:13:41,060
Mothballed rust buckets will never see action again.

201
00:13:42,180 --> 00:13:44,540
Over 70 derelict ships moor in the bay.

202
00:13:45,700 --> 00:13:48,600
cargo ships, missile cruisers, and tankers,

203
00:13:48,601 --> 00:13:52,980
most between 400 and 700 feet in length.

204
00:13:52,981 --> 00:13:57,140
These relics date from as far back as the 1940s

205
00:13:57,141 --> 00:13:59,580
and include among their ranks the distinguished battleship

206
00:13:59,581 --> 00:14:03,620
USS Iowa, launched in 1943.

207
00:14:03,621 --> 00:14:07,020
Officially, the Navy considers them reserve ships.

208
00:14:07,021 --> 00:14:10,300
Unofficially, they're known as the Ghost Fleet.

209
00:14:10,301 --> 00:14:13,120
The only battle these veteran warriors fight today

210
00:14:13,121 --> 00:14:14,220
is one against corrosion.

211
00:14:15,260 --> 00:14:17,800
We have to deal with the water and the salt

212
00:14:17,801 --> 00:14:20,360
in the water coming up and corroding inside of the hull.

213
00:14:20,760 --> 00:14:22,280
You don't want holes in the hull of the ship.

214
00:14:22,400 --> 00:14:24,400
You don't want to take water on the vessel.

215
00:14:25,460 --> 00:14:26,800
As they await disposal,

216
00:14:27,440 --> 00:14:29,500
the rusting hulks have shed over 20 tons

217
00:14:29,501 --> 00:14:31,060
of toxic metals into the bay,

218
00:14:31,620 --> 00:14:33,600
including chromium, lead, and mercury.

219
00:14:35,020 --> 00:14:36,820
But corrosion isn't just a problem

220
00:14:36,821 --> 00:14:37,940
for the aging ghost fleet.

221
00:14:39,320 --> 00:14:42,080
It's a costly burden for the entire U.S. military.

222
00:14:43,120 --> 00:14:45,080
The cost of corrosion, Department of Defense-wide,

223
00:14:45,081 --> 00:14:48,880
has been estimated to be somewhere between $10 and $20 billion per year.

224
00:14:49,980 --> 00:14:54,480
That's roughly the equivalent of what Canada spends annually on its entire defense program.

225
00:14:55,820 --> 00:14:59,480
Of that, the Navy's cost of corrosion for ships and submarines alone,

226
00:14:59,760 --> 00:15:04,020
not including aviation, is about $2.4 billion every year.

227
00:15:04,200 --> 00:15:07,960
That represents, on the order of 20 to 25 percent of our maintenance costs yearly,

228
00:15:08,400 --> 00:15:12,720
as well as sailors spend a lot of time performing corrosion prevention.

229
00:15:13,120 --> 00:15:17,860
It's a never-ending task of having the crew trying to fight this corrosion

230
00:15:17,861 --> 00:15:20,920
and scaling and chipping and painting and repainting.

231
00:15:21,120 --> 00:15:24,280
And it seems like you just painted there and now you have to paint again,

232
00:15:24,360 --> 00:15:27,040
but that's just what you have to do to keep the ship from rusting away.

233
00:15:28,200 --> 00:15:32,220
The culprit that accelerates corrosion aboard ship is salt water.

234
00:15:33,160 --> 00:15:35,680
Salt water is more corrosive than fresh water,

235
00:15:36,520 --> 00:15:41,380
and it acts as a conduit for the electrons to flow from one area to the ship to the other.

236
00:15:41,381 --> 00:15:46,560
and the areas of the ship that the electrons are leaving the iron creates rust areas.

237
00:15:48,360 --> 00:15:51,240
Shielding a ship's metal surface with sealants and paint

238
00:15:51,241 --> 00:15:53,820
is the first line of defense against salt water.

239
00:15:55,240 --> 00:15:59,320
The Navy also uses another extremely efficient means of combating corrosion.

240
00:16:01,020 --> 00:16:03,680
Credit Englishman Sir Humphrey Davy for that method.

241
00:16:04,540 --> 00:16:08,360
He attached a chunk of iron to the hull of a copper-clad British naval ship.

242
00:16:09,040 --> 00:16:10,840
It's called cathodic protection.

243
00:16:11,380 --> 00:16:15,280
and he introduced it in 1824.

244
00:16:15,281 --> 00:16:17,940
The iron, with its greater tendency to oxidize,

245
00:16:17,941 --> 00:16:21,100
reduced corrosion in the copper.

246
00:16:21,101 --> 00:16:23,800
Cathodic protection works because water,

247
00:16:23,801 --> 00:16:26,400
and the ship's metal hull in contact with the water,

248
00:16:26,401 --> 00:16:28,980
act like a battery.

249
00:16:28,981 --> 00:16:31,200
The salt water is a conductor of electric current.

250
00:16:33,940 --> 00:16:38,180
The ship's hull becomes a cathode, with current flowing in.

251
00:16:38,181 --> 00:16:42,740
The corrosive or sacrificial metal becomes an anode, releasing current.

252
00:16:43,500 --> 00:16:50,520
The flow of current from the anode to the cathode releases ions from the anode, causing it to corrode instead of the ship's hull.

253
00:16:52,680 --> 00:16:56,960
This is a 20-pound zinc anode used on the submerged hulls of ships.

254
00:16:58,580 --> 00:17:02,560
This is a similar piece of zinc after one year as a sacrificial anode.

255
00:17:04,020 --> 00:17:07,480
Today, the Navy uses an even more effective form of cathodic protection.

256
00:17:08,180 --> 00:17:10,320
called impressed current.

257
00:17:10,321 --> 00:17:13,160
This is our cathodic protection device.

258
00:17:13,161 --> 00:17:16,360
It applies a voltage to the hull of the vessel.

259
00:17:16,361 --> 00:17:19,940
It's helping to protect the hull of the ship from corrosion.

260
00:17:19,941 --> 00:17:22,800
It has six annos underneath the water line

261
00:17:22,801 --> 00:17:26,400
that 0.85 volts is applied to.

262
00:17:26,401 --> 00:17:30,220
What the system is doing is it's putting a charge

263
00:17:30,221 --> 00:17:33,740
on the entire hull, so the entire hull has an equal

264
00:17:33,741 --> 00:17:36,740
potential throughout.

265
00:17:36,741 --> 00:17:40,420
The .85 voltage is so low that it's undetectable to the touch,

266
00:17:40,860 --> 00:17:44,420
but still enough to prevent electrons from leaving one area of the hull

267
00:17:44,421 --> 00:17:46,920
and traveling to another, causing corrosion.

268
00:17:47,780 --> 00:17:50,940
Still, you can only slow corrosion. You can't stop it.

269
00:17:51,520 --> 00:17:55,660
The elements and the environment are always trying to take the ship back to nature,

270
00:17:55,880 --> 00:17:58,900
and it's our job to try and keep nature from doing that.

271
00:18:00,240 --> 00:18:04,340
In its endless war against corrosion, the U.S. Navy comes here.

272
00:18:04,341 --> 00:18:07,880
This is the Naval Research Laboratory's Marine Corrosion Facility.

273
00:18:08,220 --> 00:18:10,460
We're on the Naval Air Station in Key West, Florida.

274
00:18:10,940 --> 00:18:15,400
This is where we will test run many of our new technologies and new systems

275
00:18:15,401 --> 00:18:17,860
for corrosion mitigation in the marine environment.

276
00:18:19,240 --> 00:18:24,100
The lab's cathodic protection design facility is at the cutting edge of corrosion research.

277
00:18:24,600 --> 00:18:29,320
The tank that we're looking at here is a 30-foot diameter tank that's 10 feet deep

278
00:18:29,321 --> 00:18:31,880
and holds about 55,000 gallons of water.

279
00:18:31,881 --> 00:18:37,560
We are looking at a scale model of the Zumwalt-class destroyer.

280
00:18:37,920 --> 00:18:47,200
This is the Navy's new destroyer, and this is a fiberglass replica of the hull that's been laid out for cathodic protection design.

281
00:18:47,680 --> 00:18:52,220
The fiberglass hull is studded with steel chips to replicate paint damage.

282
00:18:52,920 --> 00:18:56,480
The water in the tank is agitated to simulate a ship's motion.

283
00:18:57,060 --> 00:19:00,860
The model gives us a very cost-effective method of doing this.

284
00:19:00,861 --> 00:19:02,360
We don't have to work with the real ship,

285
00:19:02,500 --> 00:19:05,180
and we can change component locations in real time.

286
00:19:05,620 --> 00:19:08,300
The model is scanned to determine the best placement

287
00:19:08,301 --> 00:19:09,880
of cathodic protection on the hull,

288
00:19:10,980 --> 00:19:14,000
information that will be used in the construction of the actual ship.

289
00:19:15,500 --> 00:19:18,120
This is the only facility of its kind in the world.

290
00:19:18,121 --> 00:19:20,080
We design for both the U.S. Navy

291
00:19:20,081 --> 00:19:23,520
and have done work for some commercial sector

292
00:19:23,521 --> 00:19:25,180
and also other navies of the world.

293
00:19:25,960 --> 00:19:27,180
Also one of a kind

294
00:19:27,181 --> 00:19:30,680
is the facility's full-scale ballast simulation tank,

295
00:19:30,860 --> 00:19:34,360
where researchers can study new preservation methods.

296
00:19:34,361 --> 00:19:37,260
Ballast tanks are our number one corrosion issue in the Navy,

297
00:19:37,261 --> 00:19:39,260
mainly because we store seawater in them.

298
00:19:39,261 --> 00:19:42,800
We store seawater in ballast tanks to adjust ship trim and list,

299
00:19:42,801 --> 00:19:44,500
as well as buoyancy.

300
00:19:44,501 --> 00:19:47,000
Because of the fact that there's such a large corrosion problem,

301
00:19:47,001 --> 00:19:49,220
we invest the majority of our R&D resources

302
00:19:49,221 --> 00:19:52,420
into new coating systems, monitoring technologies,

303
00:19:52,421 --> 00:19:54,860
and inspection methods.

304
00:19:54,861 --> 00:19:58,060
One of those monitoring systems is a robotic camera

305
00:19:58,061 --> 00:20:01,900
that inspects and quantifies ballast tank corrosion.

306
00:20:01,901 --> 00:20:05,400
We use it to actually take still imagery of the tank

307
00:20:05,401 --> 00:20:07,560
and actually analyze the percent damage

308
00:20:07,561 --> 00:20:09,240
to the coating within the tank.

309
00:20:09,241 --> 00:20:11,900
It's a useful device because it allows us to remotely

310
00:20:11,901 --> 00:20:14,000
assess the state of preservation

311
00:20:14,001 --> 00:20:16,740
without having to enter the tank, gas-free the tank,

312
00:20:16,741 --> 00:20:19,780
which is important for safety and cost reasons.

313
00:20:19,781 --> 00:20:23,580
To reduce time spent painting ballast tanks,

314
00:20:23,581 --> 00:20:25,580
the lab experiments with long-lasting,

315
00:20:25,581 --> 00:20:27,660
fast-curing coatings.

316
00:20:27,661 --> 00:20:35,100
Here we have a rapid-care technology that consists of a base component and a catalyst component.

317
00:20:36,040 --> 00:20:42,180
When I mix these two components together, essentially what's going to occur is an exothermic reaction.

318
00:20:42,840 --> 00:20:47,900
As the exothermic reaction progresses, the process generates a great deal of heat.

319
00:20:47,901 --> 00:20:50,240
It's going to get as hot as 275 degrees Fahrenheit.

320
00:20:51,020 --> 00:20:57,220
Now what has occurred here is we've made a liquid to a solid in less than two seconds as the reaction took place.

321
00:20:57,660 --> 00:21:05,060
With rapid cure coatings, time spent painting a ballast tank can be reduced from 200 to less than 40 hours.

322
00:21:06,620 --> 00:21:11,680
The time sailors spend in corrosion prevention also has a surprising hidden cost.

323
00:21:12,540 --> 00:21:16,320
We recruit sailors to drive highly sophisticated war machines.

324
00:21:16,860 --> 00:21:21,180
When they get to the deck plate, they find out that they're spending quite a bit of time doing corrosion prevention.

325
00:21:21,181 --> 00:21:25,400
The fact that these sailors are spending so much time chipping and painting

326
00:21:25,401 --> 00:21:27,940
has actually impacted our retention rates.

327
00:21:29,120 --> 00:21:34,260
This experimental heat induction tool might be just the thing to keep sailors from jumping ship.

328
00:21:34,620 --> 00:21:38,380
This is a tool that generates an electromagnetic field

329
00:21:38,381 --> 00:21:41,860
and causes the substrate to heat up,

330
00:21:42,780 --> 00:21:46,260
resulting in the coating actually popping off or losing adhesion from the surface.

331
00:21:46,720 --> 00:21:49,380
So it's very easy to just chip the paint right off.

332
00:21:51,180 --> 00:21:56,180
Reducing maintenance time is critical for today's Navy.

333
00:21:56,181 --> 00:21:58,180
As our fleet has gotten smaller over the years,

334
00:21:58,181 --> 00:22:01,180
the operational tempo of each of our ships has gotten greater,

335
00:22:01,181 --> 00:22:04,180
and that stresses the ships even to a greater degree,

336
00:22:04,181 --> 00:22:06,180
gives us less time to do maintenance.

337
00:22:06,181 --> 00:22:11,180
All of these things lead to problems with respect to corrosion control

338
00:22:11,181 --> 00:22:15,180
and the availability of our fleet to perform its primary function.

339
00:22:15,181 --> 00:22:17,180
Despite the latest research,

340
00:22:17,181 --> 00:22:20,180
saltwater will remain one of the U.S. Navy's worst enemies

341
00:22:20,280 --> 00:22:22,180
for years to come.

342
00:22:22,280 --> 00:22:24,680
Unfortunately, the corrosive effect of salt

343
00:22:24,780 --> 00:22:27,680
doesn't stop at the shore.

344
00:22:27,780 --> 00:22:30,820
Thanks to the damage caused by rock salt on our roads,

345
00:22:30,920 --> 00:22:33,420
these robots are assured job security.

346
00:22:36,240 --> 00:22:40,740
Since 1946, nearly 1,000 ships have been mock-balled

347
00:22:40,840 --> 00:22:43,180
at Susan Bay.

348
00:22:43,280 --> 00:22:45,740
Corrosion and decomposition will return

349
00:22:45,741 --> 00:22:47,300
on Modern Marvels.

350
00:22:50,640 --> 00:22:51,820
Automotive corrosion.

351
00:22:52,360 --> 00:22:55,620
It costs America over $23 billion each year,

352
00:22:56,000 --> 00:22:59,420
or $115 for each driver on the road.

353
00:23:00,540 --> 00:23:01,240
The main cause?

354
00:23:02,920 --> 00:23:03,740
Rock salt.

355
00:23:05,560 --> 00:23:09,520
The 16 million tons of it applied to U.S. roadways each winter

356
00:23:09,521 --> 00:23:11,940
do much more than just melt snow.

357
00:23:11,941 --> 00:23:18,940
Melting fresh water with salt creates salt water and massive corrosion problems.

358
00:23:18,941 --> 00:23:21,940
It's very effective from a de-icing perspective.

359
00:23:21,941 --> 00:23:25,940
The problem is it has damaging effects on automobiles and the infrastructure.

360
00:23:25,941 --> 00:23:32,940
Rock salt is the primary contributor to corrosion that we see in underbody areas and on the sides of the vehicle.

361
00:23:32,941 --> 00:23:37,940
It breaks down the inner metal surface and causes rust at the prone areas,

362
00:23:37,941 --> 00:23:40,640
such as door seams, headlight bezels, trunk bezels,

363
00:23:40,641 --> 00:23:43,340
rocker panels, wheel wells.

364
00:23:43,341 --> 00:23:45,440
Metallic road features like bridges

365
00:23:45,441 --> 00:23:49,720
also corrode faster due to rock salt used for deicing.

366
00:23:49,721 --> 00:23:53,420
Rock salt isn't the only means of deicing roads.

367
00:23:53,421 --> 00:23:56,660
Calcium magnesium acetate is a far less corrosive,

368
00:23:56,661 --> 00:23:59,620
yet equally effective alternative.

369
00:23:59,621 --> 00:24:03,440
But it's 30 times more expensive, making rock salt

370
00:24:03,441 --> 00:24:06,740
the deicer of choice.

371
00:24:06,741 --> 00:24:12,860
American automakers battle Rock Salt's corrosive effects at places like the General Motors Proving Ground in Milford, Michigan.

372
00:24:14,680 --> 00:24:17,960
The GM Proving Ground goes back to 1924.

373
00:24:18,500 --> 00:24:22,640
It's about 4,000 acres. There's about 120 million miles of test roads.

374
00:24:22,940 --> 00:24:30,260
About 4,000 people work at the facility, and a lot of different test labs and vehicle development, validation, and durability occurs at that site.

375
00:24:30,261 --> 00:24:38,540
And the testing process takes us anywhere from, oh, 12 months to 20 months to get a full, complete evaluation of the vehicle.

376
00:24:39,620 --> 00:24:45,100
To assess resistance to corrosion, the research team subjects cars to serious abuse.

377
00:24:45,700 --> 00:24:47,500
This is the grit trough facility.

378
00:24:47,720 --> 00:24:52,540
This is used to create underbody splash for our vehicles undergoing corrosion tests.

379
00:24:52,740 --> 00:24:58,440
It applies a mixture of grit and different contaminants to the undercarriage of the vehicle and the engine compartment of the vehicle.

380
00:24:58,880 --> 00:25:03,320
There's a mixture of sand, crushed stone, cinders, various different elements.

381
00:25:03,760 --> 00:25:07,940
And basically we're getting the same contaminants on the vehicles on test as what we see in the real world.

382
00:25:09,680 --> 00:25:14,500
Prior to the 1950s, corrosive rock salt wasn't a concern for car owners.

383
00:25:15,820 --> 00:25:22,180
Vehicles produced in 1920s to the 1930s were built on a much sturdier frame.

384
00:25:22,460 --> 00:25:25,880
Thicker metals were being used. Thicker body panels were being used.

385
00:25:26,560 --> 00:25:29,880
Less road salt was being applied in the 30s itself.

386
00:25:30,460 --> 00:25:36,560
In the late 50s, the vehicles lightened up from heavy steel to light steel to improve performance of the vehicle.

387
00:25:37,000 --> 00:25:39,520
Those vehicles corroded out much faster.

388
00:25:40,100 --> 00:25:44,940
Most consumers in the 50s and 60s only kept the car four to five years, and it was gone.

389
00:25:45,820 --> 00:25:53,500
Kurt Zebart, a German auto mechanic who immigrated to Detroit, Michigan in 1951, saw rust as an opportunity.

390
00:25:53,501 --> 00:25:58,220
He was amazed that the amount of rust on vehicles throughout the United States,

391
00:25:58,800 --> 00:26:00,480
he wanted to find a solution to that.

392
00:26:00,820 --> 00:26:02,800
And that solution for him was rust-proofing.

393
00:26:03,580 --> 00:26:06,540
Zeebart applied a corrosion-resistant oil-based spray

394
00:26:06,541 --> 00:26:09,200
to the undercarriage and interior panels of cars,

395
00:26:09,720 --> 00:26:11,720
where trapped moisture causes rust.

396
00:26:12,660 --> 00:26:16,960
By the 1970s, Zeebart rust-proofing had hundreds of franchises.

397
00:26:17,480 --> 00:26:19,900
Twilight Zone's Rod Serling is their spokesman,

398
00:26:20,300 --> 00:26:21,380
and a catchy slogan.

399
00:26:21,381 --> 00:26:24,540
Z-Bart, it's us or rust.

400
00:26:25,060 --> 00:26:30,300
The 1970s also saw American carmakers come under fire from the U.S. government.

401
00:26:31,580 --> 00:26:34,040
A federal economic report cited the U.S.

402
00:26:34,041 --> 00:26:38,100
auto industry as the largest corrosion problem by cost in America.

403
00:26:39,220 --> 00:26:43,580
Carmakers responded by introducing galvanized steel in vehicle construction.

404
00:26:43,581 --> 00:26:51,960
In galvanizing, steel receives a thin coating of zinc, applied either by hot dip or electroplating technology.

405
00:26:53,120 --> 00:26:55,960
The zinc oxidizes and forms a barrier to protect the steel.

406
00:26:57,700 --> 00:27:02,160
But if the coating is damaged, the zinc also acts as a sacrificial anode.

407
00:27:02,900 --> 00:27:06,140
It corrodes and leaves the exposed steel rust-free.

408
00:27:06,141 --> 00:27:14,660
You'll also find galvanized pipes, corrugated sheet metals, nuts, bolts, and nails.

409
00:27:15,360 --> 00:27:20,160
But galvanizing alone wasn't enough to satisfy demands to control automotive corrosion.

410
00:27:20,940 --> 00:27:31,479
In 79, the Canadian government mandated that a three-year warranty be provided to Canadian consumers on corrosion for all vehicles made by General Motors, Ford,

411
00:27:31,480 --> 00:27:34,180
Chrysler, and American Motor Corporation.

412
00:27:34,181 --> 00:27:43,060
That, in turn, required the United States to provide the same warranty on a competitive nature to its consumers who were purchasing the same vehicles.

413
00:27:43,620 --> 00:27:45,800
Things have only gotten better since then.

414
00:27:46,520 --> 00:27:50,260
Today, a consumer will keep a car on average 9.3 years.

415
00:27:50,620 --> 00:27:56,040
Far outlast the automotive manufacturing warranties, which are seven years, 100,000 miles currently.

416
00:27:57,580 --> 00:28:01,560
American cars owe their extended lifetimes to deep corrosion research.

417
00:28:01,561 --> 00:28:05,000
like that done at GM's salt mist facility.

418
00:28:05,720 --> 00:28:09,080
This facility is designed to expose the exterior of the vehicle

419
00:28:09,081 --> 00:28:12,900
to salt mist that's consistent with what a vehicle would get

420
00:28:12,901 --> 00:28:15,100
when it's exposed to coastal areas

421
00:28:15,101 --> 00:28:17,380
and also what a vehicle would get

422
00:28:17,381 --> 00:28:20,180
as it's trailing another vehicle on a salt-covered road.

423
00:28:21,300 --> 00:28:23,240
A test vehicle is exposed to this facility

424
00:28:23,241 --> 00:28:26,260
and this test input numerous times during the corrosion test.

425
00:28:26,980 --> 00:28:30,520
Vehicles will undergo 100 repeated cycles of each test,

426
00:28:30,521 --> 00:28:33,800
over 20 weeks to approximate 10 years of corrosion.

427
00:28:35,280 --> 00:28:38,840
The dedicated gravel road facility is designed to produce chipping damage

428
00:28:38,841 --> 00:28:42,000
to the lower body vehicle areas and the underbody vehicle areas.

429
00:28:42,540 --> 00:28:45,180
The facility itself is designed with a cover over it

430
00:28:45,181 --> 00:28:49,960
so that we can have consistent testing whether we're in rain or freezing conditions.

431
00:28:50,600 --> 00:28:53,020
The dedicated gravel road is 1,000 foot long,

432
00:28:53,240 --> 00:28:57,300
and we use the size and shape of stone that is optimized

433
00:28:57,301 --> 00:29:00,040
to create the most significant damage

434
00:29:00,041 --> 00:29:01,920
in terms of chipping on the vehicles.

435
00:29:03,500 --> 00:29:06,980
After more splashing, jostling,

436
00:29:07,260 --> 00:29:08,920
and general mistreatment,

437
00:29:09,760 --> 00:29:12,600
the cars are ready to rust and rust fast.

438
00:29:13,620 --> 00:29:15,760
We bring that vehicle back to the building

439
00:29:15,761 --> 00:29:18,280
and we put it in the corrosion chambers.

440
00:29:20,240 --> 00:29:24,700
These chambers are maintained at 120 degrees Fahrenheit

441
00:29:24,701 --> 00:29:26,740
and 100% relative humidity,

442
00:29:26,741 --> 00:29:30,100
which is a very corrosive and severe condition for the vehicles.

443
00:29:30,680 --> 00:29:34,280
These chambers are used to both accelerate the corrosion process in general

444
00:29:34,281 --> 00:29:37,760
and to also allow us to test both summer and winter

445
00:29:37,761 --> 00:29:40,480
and maintain a consistent corrosion rate for our test.

446
00:29:41,500 --> 00:29:43,720
Once we bake the vehicle in these chambers,

447
00:29:44,140 --> 00:29:45,980
we have an ability to look at the vehicle

448
00:29:45,981 --> 00:29:50,160
and verify that the performance of all of those components and materials working together

449
00:29:50,161 --> 00:29:52,500
ultimately meet our customer requirements.

450
00:29:52,501 --> 00:29:59,500
They verify that by literally ripping the car apart.

451
00:29:59,501 --> 00:30:06,500
The teardown process involves completely disassembling the vehicle, removing all of the interior

452
00:30:06,501 --> 00:30:12,500
trim, the steering wheel, the seats, and completely getting the body ready so that we can disassemble

453
00:30:12,501 --> 00:30:14,760
the body spot weld by spot weld.

454
00:30:14,761 --> 00:30:19,500
This process will take a matter of several days to get the body ready so all the components

455
00:30:19,501 --> 00:30:24,140
are laid out on the table so that the various design engineers have an opportunity to come

456
00:30:24,141 --> 00:30:29,440
out, look at their components, pick them up, hold them in their hand, turn them around.

457
00:30:29,441 --> 00:30:35,420
As we find issues, we change the design, change the material, or change the process to correct

458
00:30:35,421 --> 00:30:38,580
that issue before it gets into the hands of the customer.

459
00:30:38,581 --> 00:30:44,580
The challenge is to build cars that stand up to corrosion, but can also be made economically.

460
00:30:44,581 --> 00:30:48,820
I can build a vehicle that's corrosion resistant and corrosion proof, but the average customer

461
00:30:48,821 --> 00:30:50,280
can ever afford the vehicle.

462
00:30:50,281 --> 00:30:52,980
So you've got to balance cost as well as performance

463
00:30:52,981 --> 00:30:56,360
and durability.

464
00:30:56,361 --> 00:31:00,020
In a world of decay, nothing lasts forever,

465
00:31:00,021 --> 00:31:04,360
not even plastics, which have a lifespan measured in centuries.

466
00:31:04,361 --> 00:31:07,160
And if you're wondering where plastics go to die,

467
00:31:07,161 --> 00:31:09,700
it's the middle of the Pacific Ocean.

468
00:31:09,701 --> 00:31:13,540
And that is a big problem.

469
00:31:13,541 --> 00:31:17,100
The relays on most airbag systems are gold-plated.

470
00:31:17,101 --> 00:31:20,740
Gold resists corrosion on this life-saving device.

471
00:31:20,741 --> 00:31:23,900
Corrosion and decomposition will return on Modern Marvels.

472
00:31:27,700 --> 00:31:30,640
Plastic, it's everywhere.

473
00:31:30,641 --> 00:31:33,980
The world produces 110 million tons of it each year,

474
00:31:33,981 --> 00:31:37,240
and only 1% of that is recycled.

475
00:31:37,241 --> 00:31:40,180
What happens to the rest of our discarded plastic?

476
00:31:40,181 --> 00:31:43,180
Unlike metals, plastics don't corrode.

477
00:31:43,181 --> 00:31:44,820
They don't even run.

478
00:31:44,821 --> 00:31:49,740
but they do break down when exposed to sunlight through a process known as photodegradation.

479
00:31:51,220 --> 00:31:56,380
Ultraviolet radiation in sunlight causes chemical compounds called polymers to cross-link.

480
00:31:56,820 --> 00:32:01,600
This makes the plastic brittle, allowing it to break down into smaller and smaller pieces.

481
00:32:02,540 --> 00:32:10,200
You can think of it in terms of the vinyl top on a car or the vinyl dashboard in a car.

482
00:32:10,201 --> 00:32:16,260
If you don't put your armor all on it and resist the UV degradation,

483
00:32:16,480 --> 00:32:19,180
it becomes embrittled, it cross-links, and it cracks.

484
00:32:20,020 --> 00:32:22,600
Captain Charles Moore understands plastic.

485
00:32:23,940 --> 00:32:26,040
For over a decade, he studied it,

486
00:32:26,460 --> 00:32:29,700
as it collects in an area known as the North Pacific Gyre.

487
00:32:30,520 --> 00:32:32,320
That's where plastics go to die.

488
00:32:33,040 --> 00:32:36,460
It's easily 10 million square miles in extent

489
00:32:36,461 --> 00:32:39,460
and the size of the continent of Africa.

490
00:32:39,461 --> 00:32:43,460
The North Pacific subtropical gyre is a circular current

491
00:32:43,461 --> 00:32:46,460
that's caused by a high-pressure system.

492
00:32:46,461 --> 00:32:48,460
It circulates in a clockwise direction

493
00:32:48,461 --> 00:32:52,460
and pushes down near the center and creates a lower sea level.

494
00:32:52,461 --> 00:32:55,460
So you get this kind of a toilet bowl effect of currents

495
00:32:55,461 --> 00:32:58,460
that pull debris from the Pacific Rim

496
00:32:58,461 --> 00:33:01,460
and bring it into the central part of the North Pacific.

497
00:33:01,461 --> 00:33:03,460
Anything that floats will make it

498
00:33:03,461 --> 00:33:06,460
into the North Pacific subtropical gyre,

499
00:33:06,461 --> 00:33:08,460
everything from refrigerators to toothbrushes.

500
00:33:08,461 --> 00:33:09,460
to toothbrushes.

501
00:33:09,461 --> 00:33:11,760
Predominantly, what we see out there

502
00:33:11,761 --> 00:33:15,100
is broken down bits of consumer plastics

503
00:33:15,101 --> 00:33:18,020
that are now outweighing, and in some cases,

504
00:33:18,021 --> 00:33:20,300
even outnumbering the natural food out there.

505
00:33:22,800 --> 00:33:24,620
Plastics can take up to five years

506
00:33:24,621 --> 00:33:27,300
to journey from North America to the gyre,

507
00:33:27,301 --> 00:33:29,160
breaking down through photo degradation

508
00:33:29,161 --> 00:33:33,180
during their journey to create a plastic soup.

509
00:33:33,181 --> 00:33:35,800
Captain Moore and his crew aboard the research vessel

510
00:33:35,801 --> 00:33:41,240
Gweta trawled the gyre's surface with a 1 3rd millimeter mesh net to gather samples for their

511
00:33:41,241 --> 00:33:45,159
study. This is the first one of the repeat survey.

512
00:33:45,160 --> 00:33:49,000
We would expect our trawls to be something in the

513
00:33:49,001 --> 00:33:53,147
order of a hundred times as bad as they were in 1999.

514
00:33:53,148 --> 00:33:56,120
This appears to be what we're finding.

515
00:33:58,040 --> 00:34:01,640
The problem is more than just a scenic blight on this remote area of ocean.

516
00:34:01,641 --> 00:34:06,640
Plastic is making its way into the food chain.

517
00:34:06,641 --> 00:34:12,200
Millions of seabirds, marine mammals, and fish die each year by ingesting or becoming

518
00:34:12,201 --> 00:34:14,160
ensnared in plastic refuse.

519
00:34:14,161 --> 00:34:20,500
The chemicals that are transmitted up the food chain to fish that we consume is a concern.

520
00:34:20,501 --> 00:34:22,880
It's a concern for our own health.

521
00:34:22,881 --> 00:34:29,240
Research suggests that toxins found in fish that consume plastics can be linked to cancer,

522
00:34:29,241 --> 00:34:33,120
liver damage, and reproductive problems in humans who eat tainted fish.

523
00:34:34,460 --> 00:34:39,060
This stuff got a lifetime on the order of centuries,

524
00:34:39,960 --> 00:34:44,260
and no one alive today or their children or their children's children

525
00:34:44,261 --> 00:34:46,620
will be in an ocean free from this pollution.

526
00:34:47,000 --> 00:34:48,640
We've got to stop putting it in.

527
00:34:49,320 --> 00:34:51,500
Unlike plastics that slowly photodegrade,

528
00:34:53,040 --> 00:34:55,660
organic matter goes through a more rapid breakdown.

529
00:34:56,640 --> 00:34:58,060
It's called decomposition.

530
00:34:58,061 --> 00:35:04,420
Decomposition is the process by which things like earthworms, bacteria, and fungi help

531
00:35:04,421 --> 00:35:10,640
to break down the recently dead organic matter from plants and animals, recycle that material

532
00:35:10,641 --> 00:35:18,240
so that the nutrients can be used again, and also in that process releasing carbon dioxide.

533
00:35:18,241 --> 00:35:22,460
Plants grow when they take up carbon dioxide from the atmosphere and combine that with

534
00:35:22,461 --> 00:35:25,660
water to make glucose that feeds them.

535
00:35:25,661 --> 00:35:32,420
But once plants die, microorganisms begin to attack, releasing the nutrients stored within.

536
00:35:33,280 --> 00:35:35,300
New plants use those nutrients for growth.

537
00:35:36,360 --> 00:35:41,020
The carbon that was stored in the dead plants is released into the atmosphere to be used again.

538
00:35:42,920 --> 00:35:48,580
Decomposition may be essential in nature, but it has a high cost for civilization.

539
00:35:49,920 --> 00:35:53,700
25% of the world's harvested produce is lost to spoilage.

540
00:35:54,540 --> 00:36:00,720
Additionally, microbial decomposition of food results in the release of toxins, which are harmful when consumed.

541
00:36:02,060 --> 00:36:04,240
Decomposition also hits us where we live.

542
00:36:05,500 --> 00:36:13,720
Buildings fall victim to wood rot, the attack of fungi on wooden timbers, resulting in $17 billion of structural damage annually.

543
00:36:16,120 --> 00:36:23,460
But ironically, in America's garbage dumps, one place where we'd expect to see decomposition occur, it's being impeded.

544
00:36:23,700 --> 00:36:30,020
In a typical landfill, waste is brought in, it's covered, liquid is diverted from that

545
00:36:30,021 --> 00:36:31,020
landfill.

546
00:36:31,021 --> 00:36:33,940
So it's basically a dry tomb, like a mummy's tomb.

547
00:36:33,941 --> 00:36:39,800
The layers of earth that cap conventional landfills create an airtight seal, dramatically

548
00:36:39,801 --> 00:36:42,580
slowing the decay of refuse trapped inside.

549
00:36:42,581 --> 00:36:44,860
It's like the Dead Sea Scrolls.

550
00:36:44,861 --> 00:36:47,940
You can dig that stuff up later and it's still there.

551
00:36:47,941 --> 00:36:53,260
So where the science has gone now is the bioreactor landfill.

552
00:36:53,261 --> 00:36:57,720
convert solid waste into usable energy in the form of methane gas

553
00:36:57,721 --> 00:37:00,600
by introducing fluids that accelerate decomposition.

554
00:37:01,360 --> 00:37:04,440
We're constantly controlling the flow of the liquid into the landfill.

555
00:37:04,900 --> 00:37:07,740
We're trying to achieve a 30% moisture content in the waste.

556
00:37:07,840 --> 00:37:11,900
We think at that 30%, that's when decomposition will start to be accelerated.

557
00:37:12,680 --> 00:37:17,480
The most advanced bioreactor on the planet is outside Winter Haven, Florida.

558
00:37:18,160 --> 00:37:23,200
This is like the epicenter of world bioreactor landfill research is right here.

559
00:37:23,260 --> 00:37:24,460
here in Polk County.

560
00:37:24,980 --> 00:37:26,220
This is a large landfill.

561
00:37:26,380 --> 00:37:29,400
This is 3,000 tons a day of garbage coming in the door.

562
00:37:29,500 --> 00:37:30,520
That's a lot of garbage.

563
00:37:31,520 --> 00:37:35,120
About 40% of that garbage is intrustable.

564
00:37:36,200 --> 00:37:39,240
It's either paper or food waste or yard waste.

565
00:37:39,920 --> 00:37:42,380
And so the little anaerobic bacteria,

566
00:37:42,620 --> 00:37:44,440
anaerobic means they don't have any oxygen in there,

567
00:37:44,620 --> 00:37:45,860
and they eat that stuff,

568
00:37:45,900 --> 00:37:49,000
and they produce lots of methane, biogas.

569
00:37:49,840 --> 00:37:51,400
Capturing that gas is critical

570
00:37:51,401 --> 00:37:52,980
to a bioreactor's construction.

571
00:37:53,260 --> 00:38:01,160
Thick plastic sheeting lines the bottom of the bioreactor cell.

572
00:38:01,161 --> 00:38:06,260
Irrigation pipes set atop 20-foot layers of solid waste pile up over the base.

573
00:38:06,261 --> 00:38:12,100
A giant pyramid of waste builds with each tier added.

574
00:38:12,101 --> 00:38:17,520
Waste fluid known as leachate collects in the bottom of the pile and is pumped to holding

575
00:38:17,521 --> 00:38:18,820
tanks.

576
00:38:18,821 --> 00:38:23,520
The leachate is then pumped through the irrigation pipes into the waste, speeding decomposition.

577
00:38:24,580 --> 00:38:29,660
Wells sunk deep into the pile tap methane gas produced by bacteria feeding on the garbage.

578
00:38:32,120 --> 00:38:39,080
The bioreactor is covered with thick plastic sheeting that prevents methane gas from escaping and keeps unwanted rainwater out.

579
00:38:39,400 --> 00:38:43,340
What you see on the side slopes here is a 16-inch HTP pipe.

580
00:38:43,341 --> 00:38:47,980
And what it does is it takes the methane from the landfill gas extraction wells

581
00:38:47,981 --> 00:38:52,000
and channels it over to the waste-to-energy facility just north of the property.

582
00:38:52,600 --> 00:38:56,220
Over 500 homes are powered by methane derived from this landfill.

583
00:38:56,940 --> 00:39:00,940
That may not seem like much, but the U.S. Department of Energy estimates

584
00:39:00,941 --> 00:39:03,160
that if all landfills were bioreactors,

585
00:39:03,680 --> 00:39:06,820
they could supply the electrical needs of over 3 million homes.

586
00:39:07,800 --> 00:39:11,080
But accelerated decomposition also has another benefit.

587
00:39:11,081 --> 00:39:15,540
reducing the size of the solid waste by 10 to 30 percent.

588
00:39:16,420 --> 00:39:23,680
Our goal is to increase the decomposition to the extent that we can actually put more garbage in the cell while we're still using it

589
00:39:23,681 --> 00:39:25,720
so we don't have to build as many landfills.

590
00:39:26,300 --> 00:39:32,420
I think the dry tomb concept is going away and most people realize that long term getting the waste wet

591
00:39:32,421 --> 00:39:38,340
and getting it to decompose is the way to go and bioreactors are just going to become everyday common practice.

592
00:39:38,341 --> 00:39:45,640
We may also be seeing more of these little guys that can effectively recycle many hard-to-digest materials.

593
00:39:46,600 --> 00:39:51,260
They're every homeowner's worst nightmare, but they just might help us save our planet.

594
00:39:53,080 --> 00:39:59,400
Every year, 11 million tons of plastic, 10% of the world's total output, enter our seeds.

595
00:40:00,380 --> 00:40:03,760
Corrosion and decomposition will return on Modern Marvels.

596
00:40:03,761 --> 00:40:08,360
These may be the world's greatest decomposers.

597
00:40:08,361 --> 00:40:13,640
They cause hundreds of millions of dollars in damage to America's wooden structures every

598
00:40:13,641 --> 00:40:15,260
year.

599
00:40:15,261 --> 00:40:20,360
Such destructive power makes termites many a homeowner's worst nightmare.

600
00:40:20,361 --> 00:40:25,880
They're the primary target of a $7 billion pest control industry.

601
00:40:25,881 --> 00:40:30,000
But these creatures are not only pests.

602
00:40:30,001 --> 00:40:31,000
Far from it.

603
00:40:31,001 --> 00:40:35,460
They do an alarming amount of damage to buildings every year.

604
00:40:35,461 --> 00:40:39,840
But there are 2,600 species of termite at least on Earth,

605
00:40:39,960 --> 00:40:41,680
and only a few of them are known to be pests.

606
00:40:42,180 --> 00:40:46,680
Termites play a very important role in the turnover of high energy, dead material,

607
00:40:46,880 --> 00:40:49,680
and distributing that throughout a food chain or a food web.

608
00:40:51,120 --> 00:40:54,400
Termites physically break down plant matter into smaller pieces

609
00:40:54,401 --> 00:40:58,360
so that bacteria and fungi may chemically decompose the material.

610
00:40:58,980 --> 00:40:59,960
I love termites.

611
00:41:01,000 --> 00:41:03,800
I've been interested in studying termites now for almost 20 years.

612
00:41:05,860 --> 00:41:06,560
Dr.

613
00:41:06,561 --> 00:41:14,260
Jared Ledbetter's research focuses on the termite's ability to turn a seemingly indigestible substance like wood into a viable food.

614
00:41:16,600 --> 00:41:21,380
And his little friends may just be the key to world-changing advances in the field of energy.

615
00:41:21,840 --> 00:41:25,260
Termites are not able to digest wood by themselves.

616
00:41:25,261 --> 00:41:28,800
There's a role of the microorganisms that live in the hindgut

617
00:41:28,801 --> 00:41:33,380
in the degradation of wood into some compounds,

618
00:41:33,520 --> 00:41:36,520
which then can be used by the termite as a nutrient.

619
00:41:37,280 --> 00:41:39,460
In the gut of one termite,

620
00:41:40,520 --> 00:41:47,580
you may find as many as 200 or more species of bacteria and protozoa

621
00:41:47,581 --> 00:41:50,200
and organisms that you'll find nowhere else in nature.

622
00:41:50,940 --> 00:41:55,200
So we'll now take this slide of this termite microbial suspension

623
00:41:55,201 --> 00:41:58,900
and we'll get a very fundamental look at the microbes

624
00:41:58,901 --> 00:42:00,060
underneath the light microscope.

625
00:42:03,180 --> 00:42:07,780
And it is this amazing menagerie of wild-shaped organisms

626
00:42:07,781 --> 00:42:10,240
very dynamically swimming around and cavorting

627
00:42:10,241 --> 00:42:11,380
and turning and tossing,

628
00:42:12,040 --> 00:42:14,580
and so visually, very striking place.

629
00:42:14,860 --> 00:42:17,060
And I like to say that working on termite gut microbes

630
00:42:17,061 --> 00:42:20,020
is like working in a miniature Alice in Wonderland.

631
00:42:20,021 --> 00:42:21,560
It's almost psychedelic.

632
00:42:21,561 --> 00:42:26,560
These microbes may make cellulosic ethanol possible.

633
00:42:26,561 --> 00:42:28,560
That's the biofuel game changer.

634
00:42:28,561 --> 00:42:30,560
Science has a very good understanding

635
00:42:30,561 --> 00:42:33,560
of taking a simple sugar, like table sugar,

636
00:42:33,561 --> 00:42:36,560
and turning that into ethanol using yeasts.

637
00:42:36,561 --> 00:42:38,560
We know how to do that.

638
00:42:38,561 --> 00:42:40,560
But we have a very poor command

639
00:42:40,561 --> 00:42:43,560
of how we might change wood into anything.

640
00:42:43,561 --> 00:42:46,560
Is there any way to come up with a process

641
00:42:46,561 --> 00:42:48,560
to convert grass clippings or rice hulls

642
00:42:48,561 --> 00:42:53,200
or rice hulls or wood chips into a transportation fuel

643
00:42:53,201 --> 00:42:55,920
like ethanol.

644
00:42:55,921 --> 00:43:00,060
Isolating the microbes that break down wood is step one.

645
00:43:00,061 --> 00:43:03,420
By understanding that, that should give us some foundation

646
00:43:03,421 --> 00:43:05,460
on how we might engineer new systems,

647
00:43:05,461 --> 00:43:07,440
not having termites in the laboratory,

648
00:43:07,441 --> 00:43:09,620
but actually new systems ultimately

649
00:43:09,621 --> 00:43:11,120
maybe in large fermenters like you'd

650
00:43:11,121 --> 00:43:13,780
have at Miller Brewing or Budweiser Brewing

651
00:43:13,781 --> 00:43:16,520
to do this on a massive scale under very, very different

652
00:43:16,521 --> 00:43:17,460
operating conditions.

653
00:43:18,560 --> 00:43:23,160
Coarse cellulose could supply up to 100 billion gallons of fuel each year,

654
00:43:23,960 --> 00:43:26,560
replacing over 2 billion barrels of imported oil

655
00:43:26,561 --> 00:43:28,960
while slashing greenhouse gas emissions.

656
00:43:29,640 --> 00:43:32,240
But the termite harbors more treasures.

657
00:43:33,140 --> 00:43:37,820
Its microbial community may also offer efficient new ways of producing hydrogen gas.

658
00:43:38,480 --> 00:43:44,540
Some of the microbes are able to convert cellulose and other components of wood into hydrogen,

659
00:43:44,541 --> 00:43:47,900
do so at very high rates

660
00:43:47,901 --> 00:43:50,780
and are able to allow hydrogen to accumulate

661
00:43:50,781 --> 00:43:52,020
at very high concentrations.

662
00:43:52,960 --> 00:43:55,680
One termite can convert a single piece of paper

663
00:43:55,681 --> 00:43:57,840
into two liters of hydrogen gas,

664
00:43:58,420 --> 00:44:01,760
making termites one of the most efficient bioreactors on the planet.

665
00:44:02,360 --> 00:44:05,980
There are many different angles to studying the termite gut microbiology,

666
00:44:06,180 --> 00:44:08,520
many different things that it can tell us,

667
00:44:08,920 --> 00:44:10,900
each in their own right, very interesting.

668
00:44:10,901 --> 00:44:14,140
For now, the termite gut provides a safe haven

669
00:44:14,240 --> 00:44:16,400
for that microbial menagerie.

670
00:44:16,500 --> 00:44:19,940
Thanks to this lowly insect, we just might be able to

